/*  A simplified command shell, read-eval-loop over standard input.

    Anything on your PATH is launched, with as many arguments as you type. `Leave.` on a line by
    itself exits; so does end of input. `cd` is the one built-in.

    Arrows, Home/End, Ctrl-U and a history of what you typed all come from `tools/terminal.cufe`,
    which the REPL uses too. Not one line of editing is written here — that is the whole reason
    the terminal is a book and not a part of the REPL.

    Three things worth noticing:

    - There is no shell underneath this shell. `run` is fork + execvp directly —
        the program name and each argument cross to the child as separate OS-level
        arguments, so nothing is ever re-parsed by /bin/sh and quoting cannot bite.

    - `Run` here is the STATEMENT form: the child inherits this terminal. Its output streams as
        it happens rather than arriving all at once when it exits, and a program that draws —
        `less`, `vim`, `top` — has a real terminal to ask about. The expression form
        (`Define r as run …`) hands the child a pipe instead and gives back what it said, which is
        what you want when the output is DATA and exactly wrong for a shell.

    - The arguments are a SERIES, not a written-out list. A shell cannot know how many arguments
        it will be handed until it has read the line, so a list fixed when the shell was compiled
        could only ever be a guess with a ceiling on it — and this one had a ceiling of three.

    There is no list of permitted commands. A program that does not exist is a launch failure and
    says so, which is the same answer a whitelist gave and one fewer thing to keep current.

    Run it interpreted, or compile it — `cufet build tools/shell.cufe` produces
    a native binary with no managed runtime under it. The subprocess machinery is
    POSIX, so a compiled shell needs Linux, macOS, or WSL. */


/*  Does a name fit a pattern? `*` stands for any run of characters including none, `?` for
    exactly one.

    ★ Recursive rather than a loop, because `*` is the only hard case and recursion states it
    exactly: either the star matches nothing and the rest of the pattern must fit here, or it
    swallows one more character and the same question is asked again. A loop over this needs a
    backtracking index that says the same thing less clearly. */
Bind fact to matches, given (the text pattern, the text name):
    If the length of pattern is 0, return the length of name is 0.

    Define head as the characters from 1 to 1 of pattern.
    Define rest-pattern as the characters from 2 to the end of pattern.

    If head is "*":
        If cast matches on (rest-pattern, name), return true.
        If the length of name is 0, return false.
        Define shorter as the characters from 2 to the end of name.
        Return cast matches on (pattern, shorter).
    Done.

    If the length of name is 0, return false.
    Define first as the characters from 1 to 1 of name.
    Define rest-name as the characters from 2 to the end of name.

    If head is "?", return cast matches on (rest-pattern, rest-name).
    If head is first, return cast matches on (rest-pattern, rest-name).
    Return false.
Done.

/* Where the last path separator sits, or 0 for a bare name. Both separators count, so a pattern
   typed the Windows way finds its directory too. */
Bind number to last-separator, given (the text word):
    Define found as 0.
    Define at as 1.
    Define past as (the length of word) + 1.
    While at is less than past, repeat:
        Define here as the characters from at to at of word.
        If here is "/", found becomes at.
        If here is "\\", found becomes at.
        Increment at by 1.
    Done.
    Return found.
Done.

/* Whether a word is a pattern at all. A word with no `*` and no `?` is never looked up. */
Bind fact to wild, given (the text word):
    Define at as 1.
    Define past as (the length of word) + 1.
    While at is less than past, repeat:
        Define here as the characters from at to at of word.
        If here is "*", return true.
        If here is "?", return true.
        Increment at by 1.
    Done.
    Return false.
Done.

/*  ⚠ A listing hands back JOINED paths — `tools/shell.cufe`, not `shell.cufe` — so the name has to
    be cut back out of one before it can be matched against a pattern. */
Bind text to base-name, given (the text whole):
    Define cut as cast last-separator on (whole).
    If cut is 0, return whole.
    Define from-here as cut + 1.
    Return the characters from from-here to the end of whole.
Done.

/*  One word, expanded into the names it stands for.

    ★★ A word that matches NOTHING stands as itself. That looks like giving up and is the rule every
    shell follows: a pattern meant for the program rather than for the shell has to survive the trip,
    and a typo is better reported by whatever was asked for the file than guessed at here.

    ★ The directory part is kept exactly as it was typed and put back in front of each match, so
    a pattern with a directory written in front of it answers names carrying that same directory,
    while a bare `*.cufe` answers `shell.cufe`. Reading the
    current directory to answer the bare case would spell every name `./shell.cufe` — the same file,
    and not what anyone typed.

    ⚠ A leading dot is matched only when it was asked for. `*` skipping hidden names is not an
    accident of the matcher; it is the one place the pattern language deliberately does not mean
    what it says, and every shell agrees to it. */
Bind series of text to expanded, given (the text word):
    Define answer as a series of text.

    If cast wild on (word) is false:
        Insert word into answer.
        Return answer.
    Done.

    Define cut as cast last-separator on (word).
    Define here as ".".
    Define carrying as "".
    Define pattern as word.
    If cut is greater than 0:
        Define up-to as cut - 1.
        The here becomes the characters from 1 to up-to of word.
        /* A leading separator: the directory IS the separator, and cutting it away would look here
           instead of at the root. */
        If the length of here is 0, here becomes the characters from cut to cut of word.
        The carrying becomes the characters from 1 to cut of word.
        Define from-here as cut + 1.
        The pattern becomes the characters from from-here to the end of word.
    Done.

    Try to:
        Define listed as the contents of the directory here.
        /* Sorted, so one pattern gives one order twice over. The listing does not promise an order,
           and a command line that shuffles between runs is its own kind of bug. */
        Define ordered as listed sorted.
        For each whole in ordered, repeat:
            Define name as cast base-name on (whole).
            Define hidden as (the characters from 1 to 1 of name) is ".".
            Define asked as (the characters from 1 to 1 of pattern) is ".".
            If hidden is false or asked:
                If cast matches on (pattern, name), insert "{carrying}{name}" into answer.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        /* A missing or unreadable directory expands to nothing, and says nothing: the word then
           stands, and whatever was asked for it reports what is actually wrong. */
        Define unreadable as true.
    Done.

    If the number of answer is 0, insert word into answer.
    Return answer.
Done.


Pull a terminal.
    /* Prompts are for somebody typing. Piped or redirected, they would land in the output. */
    Define asking as cast terminal's interactive.

    /* Raw mode is asked for, never assumed. A pipe, a redirect, or a machine without termios all
       say no, and the answer is believed — the reader falls back to letting the terminal collect
       the line, which is what this shell did before it had an editor at all. */
    Define editing as false.
    If asking, editing becomes cast terminal's raw-on.

    /* Everything typed, in order, for the up arrow to walk back through. */
    Define past as a series of text.

    Define running as true.
    While running, repeat:
        If asking, cast terminal's put on ("cufetsh$ ").

        Define raw as cast terminal's typed on (editing, past).

        /* Void means end of input — the user closed the stream instead of saying Leave. */
        If raw is void, the running becomes false.
        If raw is not void:
            Define line as raw trimmed.

            /* Remembered whether or not it worked. Recalling a line to fix it is most of what a
               history is for, and the line that failed is the one you most want back. */
            If the length of line is greater than 0, insert line into past.

            If line is "Leave.", running becomes false.
            If line is not "Leave.":
                /* A blank line is not an error; it is just nothing to do. */
                If the length of line is greater than 0:
                    Define parts as line split by " ".
                    Define cmd   as the first of parts.
                    Define n     as the number of parts.

                    /* `cd` is the shell's own, not a program to launch. A child process cannot change
                       its parent's directory, which is why every real shell builds this one in. With
                       it, `pwd` finally reports somewhere you chose. A bad path is a
                       failure value, so a typo costs you a line of output and not the session. */
                    If cmd is "cd":
                        Try to:
                            If n is 1:
                                Define home as the environment variable "HOME" but void is "".
                                If the length of home is 0, home becomes the environment variable "USERPROFILE" but void is "/".
                                The current directory becomes home.
                            Done.
                            If n is 2, the current directory becomes item 2 of parts.
                            If n is greater than 2, state "cufetsh: cd: too many arguments".
                        Done.
                        In case of failure:
                            State "cufetsh: cd: {the message of the failure}".
                        Done.
                    Done.

                    /* Everything after the command word is an argument, however many there are. */
                    If cmd is not "cd":
                        Define argv as a series of text.
                        Define seen as 0.
                        For each word in parts, repeat:
                            If seen is greater than 0:
                                Define standing-for as cast expanded on (word).
                                For each one-name in standing-for, repeat:
                                    Insert one-name into argv.
                                Done.
                            Done.
                            Increment seen by 1.
                        Done.

                        /*  ⚠ The terminal goes back to normal for the child, and this is not a courtesy.
                            Raw mode is a property of the TERMINAL, not of this process, so a child launched
                            while it is on inherits no echo, no line editing and no Ctrl-C — `cat` would take
                            keys one at a time in silence and could not be interrupted. Handing the terminal
                            over means handing it over as found. */
                        If editing, cast terminal's raw-off.

                        /* Launching can fail — the program may not exist, or may not be executable.
                           That is a failure value, not a crash, so it is handled here and the loop
                           carries on. */
                        Try to:
                            Run cmd with arguments argv.
                        Done.
                        In case of failure:
                            State "cufetsh: {the message of the failure}".
                        Done.

                        /*  ⚠⚠ DO NOT DELETE THIS LINE BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE IT DOES NOTHING. Mentioning interrupts
                            ANYWHERE in a program is what puts that program in charge of its own — a program that
                            never mentions them is stopped by Ctrl-C, which is right for a script and fatal for a
                            shell. The mention is the opt-in; the poll is what it is spelled with.
                        
                            ★ It is usually a no-op, and that is correct. While a launched child holds the
                            terminal the interrupt is the CHILD’s, so nothing is recorded here for this line to
                            find. It earns its place when a Ctrl-C lands anywhere else — at the prompt with the
                            line editor unavailable, say — where it stops that from ending the session. */
                        If an interrupt is requested, acknowledge the interrupt.

                        /* Taken back for the next line. A child that changed the terminal and did not change
                           it back is why this asks again rather than assuming. */
                        If editing, editing becomes cast terminal's raw-on.
                    Done.
                Done.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.

    /* However the loop ended — Leave., end of input, or Ctrl-D — the terminal is handed back the
       way a program that never touched it would leave one. */
    If editing, cast terminal's raw-off.
Done.
